The first draw was red, so urn A is now the posterior choice with probability $\frac{3/10}{2/5} = \frac{3}{4}$, and urn B with probability $\frac{1}{4}$.

The red ball is not replaced. If the urn was A it now holds $2$ red and $2$ blue, so the chance of a second red is $\frac{2}{4}$. If the urn was B it now holds $0$ red and $4$ blue, so the chance is $0$.

P(\text{second red} \mid \text{first red}) = \frac{3}{4} \cdot \frac{2}{4} + \frac{1}{4} \cdot 0 = \frac{3}{8}
